      "problem": "|  | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=3$ | $Y=4$ | $Y=5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0.01$ | $0.05$ | $0.025$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.025$ | $0.01$ | $0.01$ | $0.025$ | $0.05$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.01$ | $0.05$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ | $0.01$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.1$ | $0.025$ | $0.1$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ |\n\nCalculate $P(Y-X=2)$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above.",
      "worked_solution": "Using the values in the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y-X=2) & =P(X=0,Y=2)+P(X=1,Y=3) \\\\  & +P(X=2,Y=4)+P(X=3,Y=5) \\\\  & =f(0,2)+f(1,3)+f(2,4)+f(3,5) \\\\  & =0.01+0.01+0.1+0.1 \\\\  & =0.22.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
